From u3fe90052fbf2f899 at groups.google.com  Sat Mar  7 23:49:14 2015
From: u3fe90052fbf2f899 at groups.google.com (Terrence Jorgensen)
Date: Sat, 07 Mar 2015 23:49:14 +0000
Subject: Re: Specifying Covariances with WLSMV and ordered data: can it be done?
Message-ID: <IsZEF9qQw8U.mXBune19zcAJ@lavaan.groups.google.com>
In-Reply-To: <IsZEF9qQw8U.DTR72GRr-ZQJ@lavaan.groups.google.com>
References: <IsZEF9qQw8U.DTR72GRr-ZQJ@lavaan.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/lavaan/c/IsZEF9qQw8U/m/mXBune19zcAJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Those variables are just exogenous predictors.  You don't need to treat them as random variables to find out their covariance, you can just use the cov() function.  All exogenous predictors are allowed to be correlated, even if that information isn't a model parameter (like in OLS regression), but if you want to include those estimates in your model, you can try this:

SM2B <-"
## Measurement model ##
PS ~ PB + PF + cIT + cET + crS + crR + GEOR + cADT + cAWS + SS + D
##
cET ~ COS + SIN
crS ~ COS + SIN
crR ~ COS + SIN
"

SEM2B <- sem(SM2B, ordered=c("PS"), data = FCSH, fixed.x = FALSE)

Terry

From u3fe90052fbf2f899 at groups.google.com  Sat Mar  7 23:56:15 2015
From: u3fe90052fbf2f899 at groups.google.com (Terrence Jorgensen)
Date: Sat, 07 Mar 2015 23:56:15 +0000
Subject: Re: why I got same chi-square, P value for different data using the same full model structure?
Message-ID: <Q38Kny_gplg.ABxYrPDc0fQJ@lavaan.groups.google.com>
In-Reply-To: <Q38Kny_gplg.-SRLd44Y-qsJ@lavaan.groups.google.com>
References: <Q38Kny_gplg.-SRLd44Y-qsJ@lavaan.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/lavaan/c/Q38Kny_gplg/m/ABxYrPDc0fQJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

The rest of your model is saturated.  All bivariate relationships are freely estimated between X (or Y) and M, and each of those with A, B, and C.  The only part of the model that gives you degrees of freedom is fixing the A-B-C correlation matrix to zero, but that is the same in both models.  Because all other relationships in the saturated part of the model will be perfectly reproduced (and the sample sizes of observed data in X and Y are the same), the only source of misfit in the model for X (or for Y) is from fixing the A-B-C correlations to zero, so the fit statistic (and its p value) will be the same.

Terry

From u3fe90052fbf2f899 at groups.google.com  Sun Mar  8 00:02:58 2015
From: u3fe90052fbf2f899 at groups.google.com (Terrence Jorgensen)
Date: Sun, 08 Mar 2015 00:02:58 +0000
Subject: Re: Using lavPredict on simple model
Message-ID: <03VO1GZzFRE.2GbBXXSEbH4J@lavaan.groups.google.com>
In-Reply-To: <03VO1GZzFRE.0rkdAqYh4tkJ@lavaan.groups.google.com>
References: <03VO1GZzFRE.0rkdAqYh4tkJ@lavaan.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/lavaan/c/03VO1GZzFRE/m/2GbBXXSEbH4J>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

> I would like to get predicted values of endogenous variable "D", given a 10% increase in A, and 20% increase in C (for example)
>

D is not an endogenous variable in your model.  It is a predictor of both C and A, but not an outcome in the regressions you specify in your syntax.

But generally, yes, passing a new data frame (without an outcome, but specifying varying levels of your predictors) to the predict function should return fitted values. I've never used it for a path analysis in lavaan, though -- I just know the predict method returns factor scores from a latent variable model (e.g., CFA).  But if it doesn't work with predict(), you can still create the new data frame with various levels of your predictors of D (or any outcome), and use the regression paths and intercept to add fitted values as a new variable.

Terry

