On 03/28/2015 01:01 PM, Chester Kam wrote:
> Dear all,
>
> I have been trying to simulate the skewness and kurtosis of observed
> variables. If I understand in one of the previous posts here correctly,
> simulating skewness and kurtosis using Vale–Maurelli approach (the
> method that simulateData is using) can lead to inaccurate skewness and
> kurtosis values.

This is a limitation of the Vale-Maurelli approach, which is based on:

Fleishman, A. I. (1978). A method for simulating non-normal
distributions. Psychometrika, 43(4), 521­-532.

In this paper, have a look at figure 1 (page 527), where you can see the
combinations of the skew/kurtosis values for which the method is valid.
For example, when skew=3, the kurtosis should by > 14. For other
combinations, you get approximate values at best.

There is no workaround. Perhaps, have a look at the simsem package. I
believe they have implemented different methods for generating
non-normal data based on copulas.

Yves.

